ing about what their motivations  are and then to deal with that so task  conflicts again are the most productive  form of conflict in teams and there's  three other forms of conflict that can  occur in teams and are generally likely  to hurt team performance these three  destructive types of conflict in teams  are process conflicts relationship  conflicts and Status conflicts process  conflicts are conflicts about the  logistics of day-to-day work in teams of  what time we're going to meet who's  going to do what what responsibilities  each person should have these conflicts  interestingly have been shown to be the  most negative form of conflict that can  occur in teams the reason being is that  these conflicts often represent  underlying issues in the team that if  you're going to fight about roles and  responsibilities is often because you  care about your status or your prestige  in the team but you're unwilling to say  that so then you choose indirectly to  choose the best responsibility for  yourself to gain prestige over your  teammates let's make these these types  of conflicts particularly dangerous  relationship conflicts are another  negative form of conflict in teams  relationship conflicts are about  personality clashes differences in  political beliefs people who just don't  get along these conflicts have always  been shown to be negative for team  performance and are best avoided if you  can the last type of conflict that's  been more recently pointed out in the  literature is status conflicts are  conflicts about the relative respect  that people have from one another within  the team about the hierarchy about the  rank order these types of conflicts are  especially prevalent and higher level  teams in the organizations or change  that people really care about status and  power so management teams  boards of directors etc these conflicts  are particularly difficult to work with  and often negative for performance  because people often don't voice them  directly is indirect behaviors is  engaging in process conflicts to claim a  choice responsibility to improve your  status as a gauge in a relationship  conflicts to derogate somebody to again  prove improve your own position and  because your status conflicts are so  insidious they're very difficult to  catch and very difficult to resolve as  if there's any way to get the team on  the same page to avoid status issues in  the first place and matter to be well  advised to do that one of the solutions  in the literature that's been looked at  then is how do you help virtual team  it's given the virtual teams have more  issues with conflict the face-to-face  teams and one main solution is to make  sure that you have a face-to-face  kickoff when you start working together  with a virtual team this is really  critical to give team members a few days  together to get to know one another so  they have a context and understanding of  the relationship and of the person in  case they do have a conflict over email  or over the phone they know how to make  sense of it to make the right  attributions rather than taking things  personally  if conflict isn't managed and isn't  dealt with and it transforms into a  negative form of conflict be a process  conflict relationship conflict status  conflict it can tear it into pieces  there's been research that shows that  conflict leads to psychosomatic  complaints people get burnout they get  depressed they leave the company and the  teams eventually can dissolve so the  dangers of not resolving conflict are  huge is something that managers really  do need to take seriously in the daily  life of their teams is to monitor their  conflicts and prevent them from getting  to that point  my top 3 tips for managing conflict and  organization include knowing what people  are fighting about why they're fighting  and what are their emotions while  they're fighting so to discuss each of  these 3 points in a little bit more  detail first under